**resilience or finding happiness despite hardship** — not necessarily that they are “happy about their life” in a broader socioeconomic sense.\n\n---\n\n### 🧭 **Conclusion**\n\nWhile **many individuals in these frames display happiness, joy, and social engagement**, the question asks about **“people living in Lebanon towards life”** — a broad, generalized attitude.\n\nThe frames **do not show a representative cross-section** of Lebanese society (e.g., no elderly, no working-class laborers, no scenes of economic struggle, no political protests — though mentioned in the global summary). The visible subjects are mostly **young people in social or leisure contexts**, which may skew the perception toward positivity.\n\nTherefore, while **the evidence in these specific frames supports Answer '1'**, it is **not sufficient to conclusively generalize** that “people living in Lebanon are happy about their life” — especially since the global summary suggests the video also documents economic crisis and protests.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Verdict:\n**The answer '1' is partially correct based on the visible frames, but it is an oversimplification.**  \n**A more accurate answer would acknowledge the visible joy but also the complexity implied by the broader context.**\n\nHowever, **if forced to choose only from the given options and based strictly on the visual evidence in these 29 frames**, the best-supported answer is:\n\n> **✅ 1 (They are happy about their life)** — *with the caveat that this reflects only the visible, joyful moments in social contexts, not a comprehensive view of national sentiment.*\n\n---\n\n**Note:** In a real-world evaluation, you’d want to see more frames covering diverse demographics and situations (e.g., protests, markets, hospitals, etc.) to make a fully accurate claim. But within the scope of *this specific set of 29 frames*, **Answer '1' is the most supported**."}], 'formatted_responses': ["\n=== Batch 0-30 (29 frames) ===\nFrames: /mnt/ssd/data/lvbenc