n---\n\n### 5. **People participating in protests or demonstrations**\n- **Strong evidence**: Frames 2–9 and 28–30 clearly show large-scale protests, confrontations with security forces, burning objects, and smoke. This is a dominant theme.\n- The **Global Emotions report** (Frames 10–13) explicitly labels Lebanon among the “world’s angriest countries,” reinforcing the emotional context.\n\n---\n\n### 6. **People going about daily life activities with visible emotional states**\n- No scenes show Lebanese locals shopping, eating, or commuting with visible expressions of happiness or contentment.\n- The daily life depicted is overshadowed by crisis — protests, smoke, and military presence dominate.\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe **attitudes of people living in Lebanon**, as depicted in these frames, are **not** characterized by happiness or contentment. Instead, they are shown engaged in **protests**, expressing **anger and frustration**, and living amid **social and economic crisis**. The **only happy expressions** belong to the visiting group, who are **not** representative of the local population’s attitude toward life.\n\n✅ Therefore, the correct answer is **NOT '1'**. The evidence strongly contradicts the claim that Lebanese people are happy about their life in this context.\n\n**Final Verdict: Incorrect.**\n", "\n=== Batch 30-60 (30 frames) ===\nFrames: /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/JPPMz8fEml0/frames/frame_0289.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/JPPMz8fEml0/frames/frame_0290.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/JPPMz8fEml0/frames/frame_0291.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/JPPMz8fEml0/frames/frame_0292.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/JPPMz8fEml0/frames/frame_0293.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/JPPMz8fEml0/frames/frame_0294.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/JPPMz8fEml0/frames/frame_0295.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/JPPMz8fEml0/frames/frame_0296.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos