st segment) | Large crowds of **Lebanese protestors** on city streets; some wearing gas‑mask helmets; police/riot shields visible. | Facial expressions: angry, determined, distressed; chanting, raised fists, smoke and tear‑gas clouds. | Strong evidence of **anger / frustration**, not happiness. |\n| **0014‑0027** (foreign‑traveler segment) | Small group of **visiting foreigners** (Ammar, Staffan, Thomas, etc.) – clearly not locals – gathered at a café/urban terrace. | Smiling, laughing, relaxed postures; occasional wide‑angle shots of them talking. | These happy faces belong to **visitors**, not to the Lebanese population the question asks about. |\n| **0176‑0189** (continuation of protest) | Same **Lebanese protest crowd**; night‑time lighting, banners (“THOUSANDS IN LEBANON MARCH AGAINST CORRUPTION”), occasional clashes with police. | Anger, determination; no smiles. | Reinforces a **negative mood** among locals. |\n| **0286‑0288** (beach/social‑gathering) | Mixed group of **young Lebanese (and a few visitors)** at a seaside spot at sunset. | Several individuals (frames\u202f27‑28) show genuine smiles, laughing, gesturing with a Lebanese flag; a woman in hijab smiling while chatting. | **Isolated moments of joy** among locals, but limited to a small, leisure‑oriented subset. |\n| **0289‑0298** (protest continuation) | Massive **night‑time protest**; tear‑gas, smoke, confrontations, banners about tax hikes. | Anger, frustration; intense facial expressions. | Dominant mood remains **negative**. |\n| **0579‑0588** (economic‑crisis context) | Still‑frames of **graphical overlays** (inflation chart, grain‑silo fire) intercut with street shots. | No faces, but visual cues of hardship. | Implies **widespread despair** beyond what is shown on faces. |\n| **1999‑2007** (urban night‑life) | Small group of **young Lebanese** at a city square, casually chatting; some smiling, but overall subdued lighting. | Mild smiles, relaxed posture; not overt celebration. | Suggests **moderate, situational contentment**, not a broad societal happiness. |\n| **2008‑2015** (playground & domestic scene) | **Children** on a modest playground; a **Lebanese woman** in a kitchen talking to a man; a **young couple** walking. | Children laughing, woman smiling, couple relaxed. | Shows **individual pockets of joy** despite poor surroundings. |\n| **2020‑2025** (still protest‑heavy) | Large crowd again, banners, police line. | Anger, shouting. | Reinforces dominant **protest tone**. |\n| **2043‑2053** (final segment) | Mixed footage: a few smiling locals in a street café, but also distant crowds and burnt‑out buildings. | Mostly neutral‑to‑positive facial expressions in close‑ups; background still shows damage. | Indicates **resilience / localized happiness**, but not a generalized national sentiment. |\n\n### Narrative Flow\n1. **Opening (frames\u202f0002‑0009):** The video opens on massive street protests, with Lebanese citizens confronting security forces. Their faces are angry and determined.  \n2. **Mid‑section (frames\u202f0014‑0027):** A brief cut shows a handful of foreign visitors smiling and chatting, which the VLM initially mistook for local happiness.  \n3. **Return to Protest (frames\u202f0176‑0189 & 0289‑0298):** The protest narrative resumes, now at night, with banners about corruption and tax hikes; the mood remains hostile.  \n4. **Intermittent “Joy” Shots (frames\u202f0286‑0288,\u202f2008‑2015,\u202f2043‑2053):** Scattered scenes capture Lebanese youths and families laughing at the seaside, playing on a playground, or sharing a kitchen conversation. These are brief, isolated pockets of joy.  \n5. **Contextual Hardship (frames\u202f0579‑0588,\u202f1999‑2007):** Overlays of economic data and images of burned infrastructure underline the broader crisis.  \n6. **Closing (frames\u202f2020‑2025):** Protest footage re‑dominates, confirming that the prevailing public sentiment in the video is **anger, frustration, and distress**, not pervasive happin